A||N||14||Black88GT||More computer problems....||||8||
Z||000000||Black88GT||01-03-2001||11:55 PM||||I know the O/T section has been swamped with computer questions recently, but I am at the end of my rope here.<P>My 8 month old Dell has had problems up to the wazoo.  Dells answer seems to be replace all types of hardware, everything has been replaced except for the CD-ROM drive, and I am still having the same problems.  This is a pain for me because I am not home during the day to let some service guy in to replace whatever.<P>Anyway, here is what is happening.  I start the computer, no problems.  For the first 5 min. of web browsing, checking email, whatever, the computer is fine.  Then in the middle of web browsing I get a error message, when I click OK I get another error message with a different type of box saying "An Internal Error has occurred... blah blah"  so I click OK, then I get another error message, equivalent to the first one, with the red X on it saying an error has occured so I click OK again and then the program shuts down.  This happens with Outlook express and Internet Explorer the most.  After I close down IE after the 3 error messages, I then cannot run any other program without getting an error message before or during the use of the program.  At this point, if the computer hasn't already frozen, I try to shut it down (ALT CTRL DEL) and then it will freeze, in which I will then have to just turn it off manually and then run Scandisk.  If I try to use my CD burner it will also freeze a lot, but it only does it occasionally.<P>After a while, I started to click details instead of just OK when I got the error messages.  It always says the same thing, "Invalid page fault in xxxxx (numbers) caused an error in xxx.dll (various .DLL files)"  Usually, it is either KERNEL32.DLL, or MSHTML.DLL.  I have copied and pasted all of them so far, its about 20 or so different ones.<P>So, I think these files might be corrupt.  The only problem is, I did get Jeff (Only2xess) to send me KERNEL32.DLL, but I can't replace it because it says "this file is being used by windows" or whatever. Could someone tell me how to replace these files, using DOS I am assuming.<P>I am actually lucky I got through this without getting an error message.  Usually I will get done typing a long post only to get an error and I have to restart.<P>If it matters, the windows version is 98 and the computer is 733mhz with 3 slots of 128 mb random access memory cards (or RAM, I just wanted to show off all of my computer knowledge hehe)<P><BR>thanks for any input<BR><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||8
Z||000001||RNGCRSH||01-04-2001||12:53 AM||||Get "Norton system works" and run the system check. if that doesent fix the problem.<BR>Try reloading the OS over itself. this will usualy take care of these type problems. <P>even if "system works" does not fix the problem it is still a good idea to have this type of maintenance software to keep your hard drive healthy. The maintenance software included in windows 9x is useless.<P><BR>If your computer did not come with a full version of 98, but insted came with a system disk that only works in dell computers, I would recomend buying a new copy of 98 or ME.<P>The system disk that Dell ships does not properly clean the system when it reinstall itself on top of an old load. this will cause more problems than you already have.<P>My words are ,those of experience, not fact. use at your own discretion.<p>[This message has been edited by RNGCRSH (edited 01-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||baldlobo||01-04-2001||03:10 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by RNGCRSH:<BR><B>Get "Norton system works" and run the system check. if that doesent fix the problem.<BR>Try reloading the OS over itself. this will usualy take care of these type problems. <P>even if "system works" does not fix the problem it is still a good idea to have this type of maintenance software to keep your hard drive healthy. The maintenance software included in windows 9x is useless.<P><BR>If your computer did not come with a full version of 98, but insted came with a system disk that only works in dell computers, I would recomend buying a new copy of 98 or ME.<P>The system disk that Dell ships does not properly clean the system when it reinstall itself on top of an old load. this will cause more problems than you already have.<P>My words are ,those of experience, not fact. use at your own discretion.<P>[This message has been edited by RNGCRSH (edited 01-04-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I agree with RNGCRSH but if you don't have anything of any importance on the harddrive I suggest fdisking the harddrive then installing the Full verson of windows either 98 or ME.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Black88GT||01-04-2001||04:18 PM||||I was dreading that.  You guys don't know how many times I have had to reinstall Windows.  I bet I could do it faster then anyone at Dell.  It's not too big of a deal for me now since I installed a CD burner and by the grace of God it worked enough for me to save my MP3s and stuff, so it won't be too big of a loss.  I guess I have to email Jeff (Only2xess) and get him to sell me the ME version he has...<P><BR>thanks<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||1
Z||000004||LarryB||01-04-2001||04:56 PM||||The problem you're describing would be consistent with marginal cooling of the CPU. <P>Has the CPU/heatsink/fan combination been replaced?  Have you tried running the machine with the cover off, even better if you can take a desktop fan and blow room air directly into the case with the cover off.<P>Or, since you're running a CPU with 133MHz FSB if the BIOS allows it you can reset the BIOS to use 100MHz FSB with 5.5 multiplier and see if that makes a difference. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||baldlobo||01-04-2001||04:58 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Black88GT:<BR><B>I was dreading that.  You guys don't know how many times I have had to reinstall Windows.  I bet I could do it faster then anyone at Dell.  It's not too big of a deal for me now since I installed a CD burner and by the grace of God it worked enough for me to save my MP3s and stuff, so it won't be too big of a loss.  I guess I have to email Jeff (Only2xess) and get him to sell me the ME version he has...<P><BR>thanks<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>First befor you fdisk your harddrive find a reg. cleaner see if their is something you deleted that changed those files.<P>PS: here is a link to a page that has reg. cleaners <A HREF="http://www.tweakfiles.com/" TARGET=_blank>www.tweakfiles.com/</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||loafer87gt||01-04-2001||06:20 PM||||If you are using a later release of Win 98, click your start bar, and go to Programs/Accessories/System Tools/ and run System Information. Under the tools tab at the top of the program, click system file checker. This will tell you if any of your system files have been modified and allow you to repair them. There is also a registry checker under the tools menu too that you might want to run. Hope this helps you out. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||batboy||01-04-2001||08:44 PM||||Make sure you don't have a virus, although reformatting the harddrive should eliminate that risk.<P>I wonder what motherboard is being used? If it happens to use a VIA chipset rather than an Intel chipset, you'll need to download the most recent 4 in 1 VIA driver set.<P>Also, make sure you don't have any hardware conflicts by checking in control panel, system, device manager.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Black88GT||01-04-2001||11:56 PM||||batboy, thats what I had wondered about too, but I bought the latest version of McAfee and ran it, came up clean.<P>I did notice one strange thing in the device manager.  Under the "Performance" part, it says in the white box under it...<P>"Compatibility-mode paging reduces overall system performance" on the first line.  When I click on that line and then details, I get this....<P>"Windows was unable to identify a real-mode driver or memory-resident program loaded in your Config.sys or Autoexec.bat file. You will see another message that lists the name of the driver or program causing the problem.<BR>Because Windows could not identify the driver or program, it has switched to MS-DOS compatibility mode to ensure the program will run. However, this decreases overall performance. To improve performance, remove the program or driver causing the problem, or contact its manufacturer for an upgraded version."<P>The only thing is, it doesn't list any drivers that its having the problem with...<P>Then in the same box as the first sentence, I get this...<P>"Drive C is using MS-DOS compatibility mode file system"<P>When I click details, I get this.<P>"Drive X is using an MS-DOS compatibility mode file system.<P>This problem often occurs on more than one drive. Usually it is caused by a driver listed in the Config.sys file that Windows does not recognize and that is related to the message Compatibility mode paging reduces overall system performance. If you see both messages, see the Help for the Compatibility mode paging reduces overall system performance message first.<BR>If you only see the message Drive X is using an MS-DOS compatibility mode file system on one drive, that drive is using an MS-DOS compatibility (real-mode) driver, which may reduce that drive’s performance. Contact your hardware manufacturer to see if an updated driver is available for your drive. If not, the drive’s performance may be less than optimal, but you can still use it."<P>Weird.  I also noticed that it says no disk compression is being used.  I think I forgot to compress the drive when I reinstalled it for the 10th time. <P>P.S. larry, thanks for the idea, I will try that tomorrow with a fan I have in my room... (edited for this)<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD<p>[This message has been edited by Black88GT (edited 01-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||RNGCRSH||01-05-2001||12:33 AM||||you will get the real mode driver error message if you have MSCDEX or a driver such as that, loaded in the config.sys file. or if you have DOS based disk compression. I would suspect the first. ||||reg||1
Z||000010||batboy||01-05-2001||02:21 PM||||Yes, the next step would be to pursue the disk compression thing. Pesonally, I'd never use the DOS disk compression. I've heard of too many problems with using it.<P>Another thing to try is to do a ctrl-alt-delete and look at the task manager to see what memory resident programs are running. There are only 2-3 programs that HAVE to be running in the background.||||reg||1
Z||000011||rogergarrison||01-05-2001||04:06 PM||||i have windows 98 too. I kept getting 'memory driver error'. turned out that I had picked up a virus that Norton didnt catch. I switched to McAfee when the techs fixed it for $125||||reg||1
Z||000012||GT Bastard||01-06-2001||05:07 PM||||Jeez... <P>If you're getting the "msdos compatability mode" message, check the device manager for hard disk controllers and see if they're splatted with a yellow dot.  It could really be just about anything at this point, but don't let them mess with any hardware.  It sounds like a Windows problem.  ||||reg||1
Z||000013||DJRice||01-06-2001||08:23 PM||||With Win98, unless you are using some older games or apps (or old hardware), you really have no need for autoexec.bat or config.sys<P>Also, If you have reloaded windows without formatting the HD, then you have not really reloaded it.  You need to completely wipe the HD for that solution to help you.  <P>------------------<BR>Dillon<BR>Black '87 SE V6<BR>"You mean like the back seat of a Volkswagen?"||||reg||1
Z||000014||FieroSTETZ||01-08-2001||12:53 AM||||click "start&gt;run&gt;msconfig"<BR>click the "startup" tab<BR>uncheck everything but "systray" "taskmon" and "regscan" if these dont show up, dont worry. click "apply" then "ok" say not to shut down, then click "start" "shut down" "restart" and hold shift while pressing enter/clicking okay, when the computer comes back up, try going online and see if the problem is still there.||||reg||1
